# Generate FlexFEC (forward error correction) on the WHEP sending side.
#
# Why, measured on a real line (2026-07-29):
#
# Packet loss reaches viewers as visible damage, and the two mechanisms we
# already have do not close the gap:
#
#   * NACK/retransmission works, but only if the reply arrives before the
#     receiver gives up on the gap. Over the real line the round trip is
#     ~60 ms; with a 20 ms gap budget in the player every retransmission
#     was arriving too late and being discarded. Raising that budget to
#     100 ms halves the final packet loss (576 -> 285 over 45 s at 2 %
#     induced loss) — but 1782 gaps remain. NACK alone is not enough.
#   * A key frame on request repairs the picture, but only after the fact
#     and at the cost of a full intra frame.
#
# FEC is the layer neither of those covers: redundancy sent *ahead* of the
# loss, so the receiver reconstructs without asking and without waiting a
# round trip. This is what Sunshine/Moonlight ship as their default for the
# same use case (fec_percentage, default 20).
#
# Why this belongs in MediaMTX and not in our sender:
#
# Measured with a packet capture on both directions of the same interface
# (streaming/testbench/rtp-durchreichung.py): MediaMTX passes the payload
# through packet for packet — the size distribution of the video stream is
# identical on both sides (85.5 % vs 85.5 % in the 1200-byte class, 0.5
# percentage points total deviation) — but it rewrites SSRC and sequence
# numbers. Parity generated by our sender therefore cannot survive the hop:
# it refers to sequence numbers that no longer exist downstream. A FEC
# generator inside MediaMTX, on the other hand, sits on exactly the packet
# boundaries our encoder chose.
#
# What the patch does:
#
# 1. registerInterceptors gains a `sendet` flag and configures pion's
#    FlexFEC-03 interceptor when PULSE_FLEXFEC=1, but only for the sending
#    (WHEP read) side — Publish: true in session.go::runRead.
# 2. FlexFEC is registered FIRST. pion documents this requirement in
#    ConfigureFlexFEC03: any interceptor that still touches RTP (TWCC and
#    its header extension above all) would otherwise also modify the parity
#    packets and destroy the arithmetic the receiver relies on.
# 3. The ratio is configurable. pion defaults to 5 media + 2 parity packets
#    (40 % overhead); we default to 10 + 2 (20 %), matching Sunshine.
#    PULSE_FLEXFEC_MEDIA and PULSE_FLEXFEC_FEC override it.
#
# The parity is added on top and takes nothing away from the video bitrate:
# the encoder lives in the sender and knows nothing of this, the interceptor
# merely appends packets to the outgoing stream. 4000 kbps of video becomes
# 4000 kbps of video plus roughly 800 kbps of parity. That is a deliberate
# product decision (2026-07-29) — a viewer on a tight line now carries more
# load, and overload itself causes loss, so this may need revisiting.
#
# Unset PULSE_FLEXFEC and the server behaves exactly like upstream.
#
# Builds against 1.19.1, go vet clean, package tests green. Note that the
# generator only engages once a viewer negotiates flexfec-03 in SDP; a
# receiver that does not offer it gets the unchanged stream.
#
diff --git a/internal/protocols/webrtc/peer_connection.go b/internal/protocols/webrtc/peer_connection.go
